afk: plans must expose parallelism; implementers do not commit;
Subagent-driven means parallel waves, Native stays sequential.

Motivation: execution happens as parallel waves of isolated subagents
(see skills-subagent-driven-development.patch), so every plan needs a
machine-followable map of which tasks can run concurrently, and
per-task git commit steps are meaningless — the orchestrator lands each
task by cherry-picking its parked ref (inline execution closes each task
as a jj change instead — see skills-executing-plans.patch).

What must survive a rebase:
- Plan header REQUIRES a "## Dependency Map" section: per-task
  depends-on edges derived from the Interfaces Consumes/Produces
  blocks, plus wave grouping; guidance to minimize edges (wide waves
  beat deep chains). It sits after upstream's Global Constraints and
  Review Focus sections, right before the header's closing `---`.
- Task template carries "**Depends on:**" and ends at the passing-test
  step — no commit step, no "Commit" granularity bullet, no "frequent
  commits" anywhere.
- Self-review includes the dependency check (map matches Interfaces,
  acyclic, no edge without a consumed interface), numbered after
  upstream's own items.
- Execution handoff (upstream v6.4.1: Subagent-driven vs Native, agent
  recommends one): Subagent-driven is described as parallel isolated
  subagents per Dependency Map wave with review gating each merge;
  Native is described as sequential with a `reviewer`-agent final review
  — not "the way this harness runs work" nor "fastest", and no "most
  capable model" (the task tool has no model parameter).
- Dropped on purpose: the worktree provisioning context line.
- The former plan-document-reviewer-prompt.md hunk (`reviewer` agent
  instead of `general-purpose`) is gone because upstream v6.4.2 deleted
  that template; re-add the same substitution if it ever returns.

